t's five tiles. Not enough. So perhaps the only way is to have another meld. But how?\n\nWait, maybe I can form a Pung of B9s? No, I have two B9s. A Pung needs three. So no. Or a pair of B9s and a pair of J2. Then the remaining tiles would be W2, W4, T1, T2, B2. Still five tiles. Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the J2 is part of a Pung. But I only have one J2. So no.\n\nHmm. Maybe the best option is to Pass, keeping the W3 in hand for potential future plays. If I Peng, I might be stuck with an incomplete hand. So the safer choice is to Pass and keep the option to form a Chow with W2, W3, W4 if I can get the third W3 later. But since the valid action is Peng W3, which is available now, maybe there's a chance to form a valid hand with it. But based on the analysis, it's not clear. So perhaps the correct choice is to Pass.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Pass]\nReason: Penging W3 would form a Pung but may not contribute to a valid winning hand due to incomplete melds. The current hand lacks sufficient melds and pairs to reach the minimum 8 points, and passing preserves flexibility for future draws.\nAnswer: Pass"}
