aining. Discarding B2 won't affect others much, and it's not part of any immediate meld. So maybe Play B2 is the best choice. Let me verify:\n\nAfter discarding B2, my hand is [W2, W3x2, W4, T1, T2, B4, B6, B7, B8, B9x3]. Then, possible melds:\n\n- Pung of B9s.\n- Chow of W2, W3, W4.\n- Chow of B6, B7, B8.\n- Then need another meld. The remaining tiles are T1, T2, B4. Maybe a pair of T1 and T2? Not possible. Or maybe another chow if I have T3, but I don't. So this leaves me with three tiles that don't form a meld. That's a problem. So maybe this isn't the right approach.\n\nWait, maybe I should discard T2 instead. T2 has 4 remaining. Discarding T2 would leave me with T1. Then the remaining tiles are W2, W3x2, W4, B4, B6, B7, B8, B9x3. The melds would be:\n\n- Pung of B9s.\n- Chow of W2, W3, W4.\n- Chow of B6, B7, B8.\n- Then remaining tiles: T1, B4. Not helpful. Still missing a meld.\n\nHmm. Maybe I need to discard a tile that's part of a potential pair. For example, if I discard one B9, I have two left, which can be a pair. Then the other melds can be formed. Let's see:\n\nDiscard B9, hand becomes [W2, W3x2, W4, T1, T2, B2, B4, B6, B7, B8, B9x2]. Then:\n\n- Pair of B9s.\n- Chow of W2, W3, W4.\n- Chow of B6, B7, B8.\n- Need another meld. Remaining tiles: W3, T1, T2, B2, B4. Maybe a chow of T1, T2, T3? No T3. Or B2, B3, B4? No B3. So no. So this still leaves me with three tiles. Not good.\n\nWait, maybe I should discard a different tile. What if I discard W4? W4 has 3 remaining. Discarding it might be risky, but if I do, my hand becomes [W2, W3x2, T1, T2, B2, B4, B6, B7, B8, B9x3]. Then:\n\n- Pung of B9s.\n- Chow of W2, W3, W3? No, chows need consecutive numbers. Wait, W2 and W3 can't form a chow without another tile. So maybe this isn't helpful.\n\nThis is really challenging. Maybe the best move is to discard a tile that's not part of any meld and has a high remaining count. Looking back, B2 has 4 remaining, so Play B2 might be the safest. Even though it doesn't hel