hich is better: discarding F1 to keep B6s for possible Pung, or discarding B6 to form a Chow? \n\nIf the player discards B6, they can form a Chow of B6-B7-B8, which would be a 0-point meld but helps in forming All Chows. \n\nIf they keep B6s and discard F1, they might aim for a Pung in B6. \n\nConsidering the scoring patterns, forming a Pung of B6 would add 2 points. Forming a Chow of B6-B7-B8 would be part of All Chows (if they can form four Chows and a pair). \n\nBut given the current hand, forming four Chows seems unlikely. \n\nAlternatively, the player might be looking to form a Pure Shifted Chows (24 points?), but I'm not sure. \n\nAnother thought: the player has a F1 (East Wind) in their hand. Since their Seat Wind is East Wind, having a Pung or Kong of East Wind would give them 2 points (Seat Wind). But they only have one F1. So if they discard F1, they lose that chance. \n\nSo keeping F1 might be better for the Seat Wind 2 points. \n\nSo, if the player keeps F1, they can use it for the Seat Wind pattern. \n\nIn that case, discarding a different tile would be better. \n\nSo, between discarding B6 or another tile. \n\nIf they discard B6, they can form a Chow of B6-B7-B8, which helps in All Chows. If they discard F1, they lose the Seat Wind chance but keep the B6s for possible Pung. \n\nWhich is more beneficial? \n\nThe Seat Wind is 2 points, which is better than the Pung of B6 (also 2 points). But if they can form a Pung of B6 and also have the Seat Wind, that would be 4 points. \n\nBut the player only has one F1. To get Seat Wind, they need a Pung or Kong of F1. Since they have only one, they can't form a Pung. So the Seat Wind pattern is not applicable here. Wait, the Seat Wind pattern is 2 points for having a Pung or Kong of your Seat Wind. Since the player's Seat Wind is East Wind (F1), but they only have one, they can't form a Pung. So the Seat Wind pattern isn't applicable here. \n\nSo, the player can't get the Seat Wind 2 points. Therefore, discarding F